AU  - Lindholm, Max
TI  - Evaluating Growth in Early-Stage Tech Startups : A Multi-Theoretical Valuation Framework with Simulation-Based Insights for SparkCo
JO  - Doria (University of Helsinki)
PY  - 2025
AB  - This research establishes a multi-theoretical framework to evaluate early-stage technology startups by considering their distinctive features such as significant uncertainty levels along with rapid growth potential and strategic adaptability. Using SparkCo, a mobile networking platform, as a case study, the research integrates four distinct valuation methods: The research utilizes Discounted Cash Flow Analysis (DCF), Comparable Company Analysis (CCA), Venture Capital Method (VC), and Real Options Valuation (ROV) in its valuation framework. The comprehensive approach evaluates financial outcomes through different growth scenarios while capturing non-linear scaling patterns and strategic modifications commonly seen in technology startups. The framework captures early-stage venture volatility through Monte Carlo simulations modeling diverse financial paths, user growth possibilities, and monetization strategies. The simulation-based method demonstrates valuation flexibility which produces precise financial predictions even in unstable market environments. Venture capital experts provided validation for the framework during interviews which revealed that strategic flexibility along with operational scalability and market positioning are essential components in valuing early-stage startups. The experts agreed that assessments become more context-aware when multiple valuation methods are combined to balance core financial measures with qualitative factors that influence startup achievements. This research advances entrepreneurial finance by developing a systematic yet adaptable framework for assessing startup value in its early stages. The tool serves founders, investors and financial analysts by enhancing the trustworthiness and precision of financial evaluations during early-stage venture expansion in unpredictable markets.
